---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import GuideDoc from '@/components/GuideDoc.astro';
import DocSection from '@/components/DocSection.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="The Basics 101" description="Open Source: a beginner's guide, the PESOS way.">
  <GuideDoc title="The Basics 101" subtitle="Open Source: A Beginner's Guide">
    <DocSection n={1} title={'What does "Open Source" actually mean?'}>
      <p class="muted">
        When a piece of software is Open Source, it means you can see its source code. That
        opens the door to a lot of awesome things:
      </p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>Anyone can freely read, reuse and modify open-source software, and send their improvements back to the project.</li>
        <li>Collaboration happens at scale: a whole community of shared ideas and expertise can form around a single project.</li>
        <li>You get a real chance to work on software the world depends on, and make a real-world impact.</li>
        <li>You learn best practices straight from the community, through feedback and advice from project collaborators.</li>
      </ul>
    </DocSection>

    <DocSection n={2} title="I'm sold. Now how do I actually contribute?">
      <p class="muted">
        Ahh, now that's the tricky part. Contributing to big projects can be intimidating,
        especially for first-timers: you're often dealing with large codebases, and it's
        hard to know where to even start. Then the confusion sets in. Where do I start?
        How do I navigate this? Will my work be accepted?
      </p>
      <p class="muted">
        This is exactly what PES Open Source aims to address. We help you contribute. This is where members support each other, mentors advice you and we build together, because the best ideas live in the space between minds, not inside one.
      </p>
    </DocSection>
  </GuideDoc>
</BaseLayout>
